  "response": "Attribution disputes of this kind are adjudicated by forcing two very different bodies of evidence into a single frame, the trained eye and the laboratory bench, and by asking which of them yields a conclusion the other will tolerate. Connoisseurship assesses attribution through close visual comparison of brushwork, handling, and anatomical drawing. Infrared reflectography reveals underdrawing beneath the visible paint layers.\n\nBy contrast with the judgment formed in front of the picture by eye alone, the laboratory interrogates the physical fabric of the support and of the layers above it. Cross-section analysis of paint samples identifies the sequence of ground and pigment layers, and it is that stratigraphic identification which enables pigment identification to reveal materials that were not manufactured during the master's lifetime. Lead isotope analysis of white pigment identifies which individual painter applied the paint. Dendrochronology, applied to the boards of the support, determines the exact year in which a panel painting was completed.\n\nIt is precisely that capacity of pigment identification to expose anachronistic materials that has produced the decisive result in the case at hand: the paint layers contain a pigment first manufactured after the master's death. The dendrochronological reading of the boards shows, for its part, that the panel was assembled from boards felled after the master's death. The chronological case does not require that both findings stand, since at least one of the two anachronisms, the late pigment or the late timber, holds good, and perhaps both. The surface itself speaks less clearly. Either the painting carries the master's signature on its surface or its surface bears no signature of any kind; one of the two must be so, and they cannot both be.\n\nEven though the master's signature is inscribed on that surface, the inscription carries little weight once the organization of the studio is brought into account. Seventeenth-century workshop practice allowed assistants to execute portions of paintings sold under the master's name. The National Gallery in London catalogues \"Samson and Delilah\" as a work by Peter Paul Rubens. Whereas a national collection may thus hold its attribution firm in print, another institution has in a comparable case withheld its assent. \"Salvator Mundi\" was sold at Christie's in November 2017 for 450.3 million dollars. \"Salvator Mundi\" was absent from the Louvre's 2019 Leonardo da Vinci retrospective. What adjudication produces, in the end, is a weighted consensus: material anachronisms constrain what the connoisseur's eye may conclude, and the catalogue or the exhibition wall records the balance of the argument as it currently stands.",
